# November 28, 2026: Carnegie libraries of California, part 3: the towns P to Z on Wikipedia's list (part 1 took A to G
# and Pomona College, part 2 H to O and Mills College). A batch of the pass the owner asked for to put the buildings Andrew
# Carnegie paid for on the map. Sacramento's Carnegie library is already on the Atlas (sacramento-city-library-carnegie)
# and is not repeated; no other Carnegie library in these towns was on the Atlas before.
# Left out for want of a site: Palo Alto, Porterville, Redding, Redwood City, Riverside, Salinas, San Leandro, San Mateo,
# San Pedro, Sanger, Santa Cruz's Eastside branch, Santa Monica's main library, Sebastopol, Selma, Tulare, Vallejo,
# Visalia, Walnut Creek and Watsonville, all pulled down (see the note).
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia's list of Carnegie libraries in California and its articles; the National
# Register cover document California Carnegie Libraries (1990, the Park Service's copy); the Pacific Coast Architecture
# Database (University of Washington) for San Diego, Santa Ana and San Bernardino; the Los Angeles Public Library's page on
# Andrew Carnegie and its libraries; the Sonoma County Library's history of Santa Rosa's Carnegie library; and the own
# pages of the museums that use the buildings.
# Pins: ArcGIS geocoder points for street addresses and street corners, checked against Wikipedia's points where it gives
# them, and Wikipedia's point for San Jose's main library; every pin looked at on the aerial.
