  "Carnegie granted Pacific Grove $10,000 on March 12, 1906, and the Pacific Improvement Company gave the land. The McDougall Brothers drew it (the 1990 state survey names Benjamin McDougall, who also drew the Carnegie libraries of Hanford and Visalia) in a Mission style with classical touches. The cornerstone was laid on November 9, 1907, and the library opened on May 12, 1908. The survey found it still the public library, downtown, and said it came through the 1989 earthquake with little or no damage.",
